  from which comrak is derived). The licence requires that binary distributions reproduce this
  notice and disclaimer: the release artefacts of the native module must ship it.
- finl_unicode 1.5.0 (a dependency of comrak): (MIT OR Apache-2.0) AND Unicode-DFS-2016; the Unicode
  licence of its data files is in the crate (LICENSE-UNICODE), to be shipped the same way.
- syntect 5.3.0 (MIT) and two-face 0.5.2 (MIT OR Apache-2.0, bat 0.26.1): syntax highlighting. two-face embeds
  syntax definitions (from the `bat` project, themselves from Sublime Text packages) under their
  own licences. Audit of 2026-10-21, superseded by the generated page below (ADR 0022; made from
  `two_face::acknowledgement::listing()` of two-face 0.4.5, the version locked then): 59 licence files cover the embedded syntaxes: MIT (48), Apache-2.0 (8:
  Crystal, Jsonnet, Kotlin, Lean, NSIS, SLS, SystemVerilog, TypeScript), BSD-2-Clause (2: Org mode,
  Apache conf) and BSD-3-Clause (1: HTML (Twig)). No copyleft, no custom or non-commercial
  licence, no entry of type "Sublime". All of them require the copyright notice and licence text
  to accompany binary distributions: the release artefacts of the native module must ship the full
  text. State of 2026-11-18: `lua tools/run.lua gen-rust-licences` reproduces it, in
  packages/doc/native/LICENSES-rust.md, from `two_face::acknowledgement::listing()` of the locked
  version (the crate ships the texts only inside a compressed dump, so the task reads them with a
  helper, packages/doc/native/examples/acknowledgements.rs, which uses crates the module already
  depends on; the Markdown page `acknowledgements_full.md` of two-face is a page of its repository,
  not a file of the crate). The locked two-face 0.5.2 (bat 0.26.1) lists 66 licence files, more than
  the audit above: MIT (50), Apache-2.0 (13), BSD-2-Clause (2, one of them tagged FreeBSD by the
  crate) and BSD-3-Clause (1); still no copyleft, custom, non-commercial or "Sublime" entry. The
  themes of two-face are not used. The default Sublime
  packages bundled by syntect itself are not used either (the `two_face::syntax::extra_newlines`
  set is).
Test data, not shipped: the examples of the CommonMark specification 0.31.2
(https://github.com/commonmark/commonmark-spec, CC BY-SA 4.0) and of the GFM specification
0.29.gfm.13 (https://github.com/github/cmark-gfm) are downloaded by `fetch-fixtures doc` for
the tests (packages/doc/tests/external.lua) and git-ignored.
The notices of the whole crate tree are generated, not hand-written: `lua tools/run.lua
gen-rust-licences` writes one page per Rust native module (packages/doc/native/LICENSES-rust.md,
packages/json/native/LICENSES-rust.md, packages/net/native/LICENSES-rust.md) with, for every crate of
the locked normal dependency graph (the targets of deny.toml), its name, version, declared SPDX
expression, the licence it relies on (the first alternative of an `OR` that the allow list of deny.toml accepts, else
the first permissive one) and the text of
its licence files (LICENSE*, COPYING*, NOTICE*, UNICODE*, ...), identical texts once. A crate that
declares an SPDX identifier and ships no file (mlua-sys and mlua_derive) gets the standard SPDX text,
which the page says; a crate without a permissive choice or without any text fails the task. The
pages are committed; `gen-rust-licences --check` (CI static checks) and `check-licenses` fail when
a Cargo.lock changed without regenerating its page. These pages, not the lists above, are the
reference. The binary release artefacts of the native modules must ship the page of their module:
that remains a release-time item (roadmap, release automation), done when the binary rocks exist.
The Rust crates are build tools of the documentation module: the sites it generates do not bundle
them and their licences are not on the licences page of a generated site.


